## Context reconstruction

This is likely about a paper (NeurIPS 2024, ID 93022) - possibly "Fast and Sample-Efficient Simulations of..." or a surrogate/forward simulation model (e.g., a learned MD surrogate like a score-based or flow-based model on peptides). "Modelo de simulación directa" = "forward simulation model" - a model that generates trajectories directly (fast), compared against reference all-atom MD for tetrapeptides ("YVGR" or similar from AlphaFold-style datasets? Actually tetrapeptides likely from a benchmark). 

Key design elements:

### 1. Data/inputs needed
- Reference MD trajectories: tetrapeptide all-atom, with coordinates (e.g., .xtc/.dcd + topology .pdb). Need to know number of replicas, total sampled length, time step, forcefield, temperature.
- Forward model sampled trajectories: same topology/state, "sampled trajectories" from the forward simulation model - need to know how many trajectories, length.
- Topology/atom indexing to compute torsions.

### 2. Collective variables
- Backbone torsions: phi (C-N-CA-C) and psi (N-CA-C-N) per residue. For a tetrapeptide, there are typically N_res-1 = 3 phi and 3 psi (excluding terminal residues whose phi/psi may not be defined — psi of N-terminal, phi of C-terminal are undefined; typically 2 well-defined pairs per residue i=2..N-1 for internal residues). Actually for a tetrapeptide of 4 residues: phi_{i} defined for residues 2..N (using C of residue i-1, N, CA, C of residue i), psi_i defined for residues 1..N-1. Internal well-defined: phi_2, phi_3; psi_2, psi_3. That gives 4 backbone dihedrals, though some papers compute all possible including omega.
- Sidechain torsions: chi1, chi2, ... per residue with sidechains (all residues except Gly/Ala may lack them). Tetrapeptides typically chosen to have all residues with sidechains? Need assumption.
- TICA components: time-lagged independent component analysis on featurized trajectories (e.g., using all torsions as features, or pairwise distances). "Top TICA components" - number of components to compare, e.g., top 5 or the number that captures ~90% eigenvalue sum.

### 3. Similarity metrics
- 1D marginal distributions per torsion: KS test (but periodic - need to unwrap/handle circular), Jensen-Shannon divergence, Wasserstein/EMD (Earth mover distance) on circular domain.
- For TICA components: compare 1D marginal distributions (histogram overlap, JS divergence, Wasserstein) and joint 2D distributions perhaps via histogram overlap (Jensen-Shannon over 2D grids) between top components.
- Density overlap metrics: e.g., (1/√2)∫√(p q) (Bhattacharyya coefficient), or overlapping coefficient (OVL).
- Statistical threshold for "similar": e.g., JS < some value, or KS p-value > 0.05 (with caveats on large samples - p-values become trivially significant; better to report effect sizes).

### 4. Aggregation
- Per-category aggregation: mean/median across torsions, fraction exceeding threshold.
- Overall conclusion: e.g., "similar" if all categories pass and TICA components overlap.

### 5. Assumptions and decisions
- Subsampling to equalize effective sample size: since MD trajectories are temporally correlated (autocorrelation ~ relaxation times), effective sample size smaller. Forward model trajectories may be i.i.d. resampled. Strategy: subsample forward samples to match number of independent MD samples (effective N from autocorrelation / block averaging), or use fixed number (e.g., 10k samples per model) with proper thinning.
- Angular periodicity: use circular statistics - compute dihedrals modulo 2π, choose difference metric d(θ1,θ2) = arccos(cos θ1 cos θ2 + sin θ1 sin θ2), or map to (cos θ, sin θ). For KS on circular data, use a circular version (Watson test) or unwrap with appropriate branch. Simplest robust: embed angles on the circle (cos, sin) and compute metrics on those, or use a shift-invariant metric.
- Uncertainty across replicas: bootstrap over independent MD chunks / replicas to get CI of each similarity metric; only declare "similar" if within CI.
- Acceptable similarity criterion: define a concrete threshold, e.g., normalized Wasserstein ≤ some fraction of scale (e.g., ≤ 0.1 rad for torsions, in units of standard deviation), JS ≤ 0.05 nats for normalized densities, OVL ≥ 0.9.
- Number of TICA components: e.g., top 5 or components capturing top eigenvalues; time lag for TICA needs selecting (e.g., ~1-2× slowest relaxation, or default 100 steps). Since we compare distributions of projection onto components, both models should use the SAME TICA model fitted on... options: fit TICA on combined or on reference only. Decision: fit on reference MD only, then project forward samples (so axes fixed); or fit on combined concatenated data to avoid domain bias. Better: fit on reference (ground truth sets the axes), optionally also check with fit-on-combined as robustness.

